NAME
       openssl - OpenSSL command line tool for certificates, digests and random data

SYNOPSIS
       openssl x509 -in FILE [-noout] [-text] [-subject] [-issuer] [-dates]
                    [-startdate] [-enddate] [-serial] [-fingerprint [-sha256]]
                    [-ext NAME] [-checkend SECONDS]
       openssl verify [-CAfile FILE] CERTFILE...
       openssl dgst [-sha256 | -sha512 | -sha1 | -md5] [FILE...]
       openssl rand -hex | -base64 NUM
       openssl version

DESCRIPTION
       openssl is a toolbox for cryptography. Each job is a subcommand. On the
       practice servers these subcommands are available (the others say they
       are not simulated). The certificates in the Security+ labs are made for
       practice: their fields can be read, but they hold no real keys.

       x509 reads a certificate in PEM form (the text between BEGIN
       CERTIFICATE and END CERTIFICATE lines) and prints its parts: who it was
       issued to (the subject), who signed it (the issuer), when it is valid,
       its key size and its extensions (Subject Alternative Name, Key Usage,
       Extended Key Usage, Basic Constraints, where to check revocation).

       verify checks a certificate the way a client would: is it within its
       dates, and does it chain to a certificate you trust (the -CAfile)? It
       prints FILE: OK, or the error and "verification failed".

       dgst prints the message digest (hash) of files, like sha256sum.

       rand prints NUM random bytes as hexadecimal or base64 (for keys, salts
       and passwords).

OPTIONS
       -in FILE
              The certificate file to read (x509).

       -noout Do not print the certificate itself, only what was asked for.

       -text  Print every field in full.

       -subject, -issuer
              Print the subject or the issuer name.

       -dates, -startdate, -enddate
              Print the validity dates (notBefore, notAfter).

       -serial
              Print the serial number.

       -fingerprint [-sha256]
              Print the certificate's fingerprint (SHA-1 unless -sha256).

       -ext NAME
              Print one extension: subjectAltName, basicConstraints or
              extendedKeyUsage.

       -checkend SECONDS
              Say whether the certificate expires within SECONDS; the exit
              status is 1 if it will, 0 if it will not.

       -CAfile FILE
              The trusted CA certificate(s) for verify.

       -sha256, -sha512, -sha1, -md5
              The digest algorithm for dgst (SHA-256 by default).

EXIT STATUS
       0 when the command worked (verify: the certificate is good); not 0
       otherwise.

EXAMPLES
       Who is this certificate for, and until when?

           openssl x509 -in /etc/pki/tls/certs/web.pem -noout -subject -enddate

       Does it chain to our CA?

           openssl verify -CAfile /root/ca.pem /etc/pki/tls/certs/web.pem

       Will it expire in the next 30 days?

           openssl x509 -in web.pem -noout -checkend 2592000

       A SHA-256 digest and 16 random bytes:

           openssl dgst -sha256 /root/report.pdf
           openssl rand -hex 16

SEE ALSO
       sha256sum(1), ssh-keygen(1), update-crypto-policies(8)
